$DIR/main.p:15:2: p is dropped at the end of its block here, but a view of what it holds (p, line 13) was stored in G at line 13, where the compiler cannot check its uses
    An owner frees what it holds when it is dropped, at the end of its block or
    when it is given a new value, and that ends every view of that memory
    (language spec section 18.3). The view of p made by p at line 13 went into
    memory the compiler does not follow, so it cannot check that the view is not
    used after line 15.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Keep the view in a local variable or a field of one, where the compiler
    follows it, or do not let its block end while the view is kept
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3314" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:13:3: cannot store (*int)(p) in package variable G, which outlives p
    (*int)(p) points into the object p refers to, which p keeps alive in f. p
    (declared at main.p:12) is an owned[int]: the object it refers to lives only
    while p holds it, and is dropped when p is dropped at the end of its scope
    in f, or released when it is the last reference. A pointer into the object,
    or an interface that lends it, is a view that may not outlive p. When f
    returns, p is dropped and the object freed, but package variables live for
    the whole program, so package variable G would be left pointing at reclaimed
    memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store p itself, changing the type of G to match: an owned box moves there,
    and a shared or synced one is counted:
        G = p
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)
